    "updated_at": "2024-10-09T15:35:13Z",
    "body": "To back up @karlt and @zcorpan, not firing an `\"error\"` event on `HTMLMediaElement` is intentional; you can always add another `<source>` child to the media element, and the resource selection algorithm will continue. `.error` is a sticky state; once the media element is in that state, it requires a call to `.load()` to get out of it, which would start the resource selection algorithm from scratch on the first `<source>` rather than the _next_ `<source>`.\r\n\r\nCurrently, authors have to add a `\"error\"` listener to the last `<source>` to detect that none of the sources were playable: From [html](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#the-source-element):\r\n\r\n> Example: If the author isn't sure if user agents will all be able to render the media resources provided, the author can listen to the [error](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/indices.html#event-error) event on the last [source](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#the-source-element) element and trigger fallback behavior:\r\n\r\nPerhaps we could make this easier by adding a new event and property to `HTMLMediaElement` to expose the `Waiting:` state of the [resource selection algorithm](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/media.html#concept-media-load-algorithm) to the author. But that event probably shouldn't be `\"error\"`.",
